Hi Omar,

Thanks for the detailed repro and for flagging this clearly.

1. Known issue: Yes, this is a known issue in Jac 0.10.2. When a node is deleted, its incident edges are not fully cleaned from the adjacency structures of former neighbors, so [-->] traversal can return stale/duplicate edge references. We have this tracked internally.

2. Workaround: Until the fix lands, you can guard traversal results by deduplicating and filtering edges whose source or target no longer exists. A minimal pattern:

   seen := {};
   for e in A[-->] {
       if (e not in seen and e.source != null and e.target != null) {
           seen.add(e);
           // process e
       }
   }

   This should be safe to ship in production and avoids relying on stale references.

3. GitHub issue: Yes, please do file one with your full repro snippet. It will help us track priority and link any related cases. Please tag it with version 0.10.2 and "edge traversal" / "node deletion" so it's easy to find. We'll cross-reference it with the internal fix.

We're targeting the cleanup-on-delete fix for the next patch release. I'll follow up here once a build with the fix is available for you to validate.

Thanks again for the clear report and for offering to contribute the issue.

Best,
Jaseci Team
